def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: Neolithic and Bronze Age worlds
E+=[
X('nevali-cori','Nevalı Çori: a stone-age cult building now under a reservoir',H,'confirmed',2,-8400,['archaeological'],
 "Nevalı Çori was an early Neolithic settlement on the middle Euphrates in Şanlıurfa Province, south-eastern Turkey, dated to the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B, about 8400–8100 BCE. It is one of the 'Taş Tepeler' sites, together with the earlier Göbekli Tepe. A team from the University of Heidelberg led by Harald Hauptmann excavated it from 1983 to 1991 as a rescue dig before the Atatürk Dam was built; the site has since been flooded. Finds include a cult building with a terrazzo-like lime floor, some of the earliest known life-size limestone sculptures, including a bare human head with a snake-like tuft and a bird statue, skulls deposited in houses, and some of the oldest domesticated einkorn wheat.",
 [('Temple or meeting house?','The excavators interpreted the special building as a cult or temple building, a reading widely accepted alongside Göbekli Tepe; some archaeologists prefer neutral terms such as communal or special buildings, since what happened inside is not recorded.'),
  ('Lost to the water','Like many sites in the area, Nevalı Çori was excavated only because a dam was coming; the parts not dug are now beyond reach under the reservoir, so its full layout will never be known.')],
 ['wiki:Nevalı_Çori','wiki:Taş_Tepeler'],img='File:Urfa museum Nevalı Çori Diorama 4862.jpg',end=-8100),
X('durrington-walls','Durrington Walls: the giant henge next door to Stonehenge',H,'confirmed',2,-2800,['archaeological','geophysical survey'],
 "Durrington Walls is a large Neolithic settlement and later henge enclosure 3.2 km north-east of Stonehenge in Wiltshire, England. Excavations by a University of Sheffield team in 2004–2006 found houses, and it has been suggested the settlement may once have held up to about 1,000 houses and perhaps 4,000 people, which would have made it briefly one of the largest settlements in northern Europe. A timber circle was oriented towards the midwinter-solstice sunrise, and a paved avenue led to the River Avon, which connected it with Stonehenge and its own avenue. In 2020 a geophysical survey identified a ring of large pits about 2 km across around the site.",
 [('Land of the living and the dead','The archaeologists who excavated the site proposed that Durrington, with its timber circle, was a place for the living and for feasting, complementary to the stone circle of Stonehenge as a monument to the dead; others treat this pairing as an attractive but unproven interpretation.'),
  ('Britain\'s largest monument?','If the 2020 interpretation of the pit ring as a deliberately dug Neolithic boundary is correct, it would be Britain\'s largest prehistoric monument; some researchers argue that many of the pits are natural sinkholes, and the question is still being tested.')],
 ['wiki:Durrington_Walls','wiki:Stonehenge_Riverside_Project'],img='Durrington Walls',end=-2100,dateNote='Settled for about 500 years, beginning between c. 2800 and 2100 BCE'),
X('banpo','Banpo: a Neolithic village made to fit a theory of matriarchy',H,'confirmed',3,-4500,['archaeological'],
 "Banpo is a Neolithic site in the Yellow River valley east of Xi'an, China, discovered in 1953 and belonging to the first phase of the Yangshao culture (c. 5000–3000 BCE). It was a moated village of round and semi-subterranean houses of mud and timber with thatched roofs, with cemeteries and pottery kilns outside the ditch; population estimates range from about 100 to 500. Its painted pottery, decorated with geometric designs, fish and human-like faces, is among the best-known Neolithic art of China, and the site became one of China's first on-site museums.",
 [('A matriarchy?','At the time of excavation, Friedrich Engels\' theory that early societies were matriarchal shaped Chinese archaeology, and the excavators presented Banpo as matrilineal, citing gender-segregated collective burials and a lavish burial of a young girl.'),
  ('A re-reading','Later scholars argue that the evidence does not demonstrate matrilineal organisation and that the interpretation reflected the political doctrine of the time; early Banpo society is now generally described as broadly egalitarian.')],
 ['wiki:Banpo','wiki:Yangshao_culture'],img='Banpo',dateNote='Yangshao culture c. 5000–3000 BCE; Banpo belongs to its first phase'),
X('talheim-death-pit','The Talheim death pit: a Stone Age massacre in Germany',H,'confirmed',2,-5000,['archaeological','osteological','isotopic'],
 "In 1983 a mass grave of about 5000 BCE was found at Talheim in south-west Germany, in a settlement of the Linear Pottery (LBK) culture, Europe's first farmers. It held 34 people: 16 children, nine men, seven women and two adults of unknown sex. Their skulls show wounds from the sharp and blunt edges of LBK stone adzes and a few from arrows, and the lack of defensive injuries suggests they were killed while fleeing. The weapons and the skeletons point to an attack by other LBK people, and it is one of the earliest clear pieces of evidence for organised violence in Neolithic Europe.",
 [('Why?','Researchers suggest motives including land and resources in a time of crisis, vengeance, or the capture of women; isotope studies found fewer local women than men and children among the dead, which some interpret as women being taken captive rather than killed. These remain interpretations.'),
  ('Peaceful first farmers?','Talheim, together with similar LBK sites such as Schöneck-Kilianstädten and Asparn-Schletz, challenged an older picture of the first European farmers as peaceful; others caution against generalising from a handful of sites.')],
 ['wiki:Talheim_Death_Pit','wiki:Linear_Pottery_culture'],img='File:Infotafel Massaker von Talheim.JPG'),
X('ness-of-brodgar','The Ness of Brodgar: painted Stone Age temples, reburied in 2024',H,'confirmed',2,-3500,['archaeological'],
 "The Ness of Brodgar is a Neolithic complex on a strip of land between the Ring of Brodgar and the Stones of Stenness in Orkney, Scotland. Excavated from 2003 to 2024, it revealed large stone buildings, a wall about 6 metres thick and a building described as a Neolithic temple, with activity from about 3500 BCE. Some stones were painted red, yellow and orange with ochre, the earliest evidence in Britain of painted buildings, and the site produced stone roof tiles and hundreds of decorated stones. Around 2400–2200 BCE the large Structure 10 was 'closed' in a ceremony that left the shin bones of about 400 cattle. To protect it from crumbling, the site was reburied in August–September 2024.",
 [('Centre of Neolithic Britain?','Some archaeologists argue that Orkney was a source of innovations, such as grooved-ware pottery and the layout of later monuments, that spread south to Stonehenge; others see the influences as flowing in more than one direction.'),
  ('A temple?','The word temple reflects the excavators\' view that the buildings were ceremonial rather than ordinary houses; their exact use is still debated.')],
 ['wiki:Ness_of_Brodgar','wiki:Heart_of_Neolithic_Orkney'],img='Ness of Brodgar',end=-2200),
X('yamnaya-steppe','The Yamnaya: steppe herders whose genes reached from Ireland to India',H,'confirmed',1,-3300,['archaeological','genetic','linguistic'],
 "The Yamnaya, or Pit Grave, culture of the Pontic–Caspian steppe (c. 3300–2600 BCE) buried its dead in pits under mounds (kurgans) and lived by herding, using wheeled carts and wagons and, there is evidence, riding horses. Ancient-DNA studies since 2015 have shown that people with Yamnaya-related 'steppe ancestry' spread rapidly into Europe, where they contributed much of the ancestry of the Corded Ware culture, and their descendants later reached Central and South Asia. Many linguists link them to speakers of Proto-Indo-European, the ancestor of languages from English and Russian to Persian and Hindi.",
 [('Kurgan hypothesis vs alternatives','The widely accepted Kurgan (steppe) hypothesis places the Proto-Indo-European homeland on the steppe; the Anatolian hypothesis places it with early farmers in Anatolia, and some scholars propose a homeland south of the Caucasus. Genetic results have strengthened the steppe view without settling every detail.'),
  ('How it happened','How the spread took place is debated: through migration of whole communities, through warrior bands, or through disease. Some studies have found plague bacteria in Bronze Age steppe remains and suggested an epidemic eased the expansion; this remains a hypothesis.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature14317','wiki:Yamnaya_culture','wiki:Kurgan_hypothesis'],img='Yamnaya culture',end=-2600),
X('taosi-observatory','Taosi: a walled city and a 4,000-year-old solar observatory in China?',H,'disputed',2,-2300,['archaeological','archaeoastronomical'],
 "Taosi in southern Shanxi, China, is a large late Longshan site (c. 2300–1900 BCE) surrounded by a rammed-earth wall enclosing about 280 hectares, with an inner wall separating elite and commoner areas. In 2003–2004 archaeologists from the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences uncovered a semicircular structure of rammed-earth pillars with narrow gaps, and a central observation point. They argue that sunrises at the solstices and other key dates could be seen through the slots, making it the oldest known observatory in East Asia.",
 [('Observatory','Supporters, including field tests of sunrise alignments, see it as a calendar device; the scholar David Pankenier suggests that keeping such astronomical calendars may even have driven the invention of writing in China.'),
  ('Caution','Other researchers note that the structure survives only as foundations, so the original height and gaps must be reconstructed, and that alignments can be found by chance; Chinese tradition links Taosi with the sage-king Yao, an identification that cannot be tested without written records.')],
 ['wiki:Taosi'],img='File:Excavation of Observatory at Taosi (10099227926).jpg',end=-1900),
X('dholavira','Dholavira: the Indus city that harvested rain in stone reservoirs',H,'confirmed',2,-2650,['archaeological'],
 "Dholavira, on Khadir Bet island in the Great Rann of Kutch, Gujarat, is one of the five largest cities of the Indus (Harappan) Civilisation and was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2021. A local resident, Shambhudan Gadhvi, brought it to official attention in the 1960s. The 47-hectare city lay between two seasonal streams that were dammed at several points, and it had a system of channels and large stone-built reservoirs to collect and store rainwater, among the earliest such systems known. Occupation is usually dated from about 2650 BCE, with decline after 2100 BCE and final abandonment around 1450 BCE, though some recent research proposes occupation from about 3500 BCE. The city was hit by earthquakes, including a severe one around 2600 BCE.",
 [('Earliest water engineering?','The Archaeological Survey of India excavators praise its water system as advanced hydraulic engineering for the third millennium BCE; comparisons over which ancient system came first depend on how such structures are defined and dated.'),
  ('The Indus signboard','Dholavira is also known for a large inscription of ten Indus signs, sometimes called a signboard, found fallen near a gateway. Since the Indus script is undeciphered, what it said is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Dholavira','wiki:Indus_Valley_Civilisation'],img='Dholavira',end=-1450),
X('watson-brake','Watson Brake: mounds older than the pyramids, built by hunter-gatherers',H,'confirmed',2,-3500,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Watson Brake, near Monroe in northern Louisiana, is an oval of eleven earthen mounds up to 7.6 m high, joined by ridges into a ring nearly 270 m across. A 1997 paper in Science by Joe Saunders and colleagues dated it to about 5,400–5,000 years ago, making it the oldest known mound complex in North America, older than Egypt's pyramids or Stonehenge, and about 1,900 years older than Poverty Point. It was built in stages over centuries by mobile hunter-gatherers, apparently used as a base from summer to autumn, and was abandoned around 2800 BCE.",
 [('Overturning an assumption','Before the dating, many archaeologists assumed that large earthworks required farming, settled villages and chiefs; Watson Brake showed that hunter-gatherers could organise such projects, a pattern echoed by Göbekli Tepe in Turkey.'),
  ('Why build?','Saunders\'s team suggested that building episodes coincided with periods of unpredictable rainfall linked to El Niño; the mounds\' purpose, whether ceremonial, territorial or practical, is not known.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.277.5333.1796','wiki:Watson_Brake'],img='Watson Brake',end=-2800),
]
# --- part 2: the Roman and late-antique world and its edges
E+=[
X('boudican-revolt','Boudica\'s revolt (60–61 CE): the queen who burned Roman London',H,'confirmed',1,60,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Around 60–61 CE Boudica, queen of the Iceni of what is now Norfolk, led a rising of British tribes against Roman rule. According to the Roman historian Tacitus, when her husband Prasutagus died the Romans ignored his will, seized his kingdom, flogged Boudica and raped her daughters. The rebels destroyed Camulodunum (Colchester), Londinium (London) and Verulamium (St Albans); archaeologists find a thick red layer of burnt debris under Roman London dated to about this time. The governor Gaius Suetonius Paulinus defeated the Britons in a final battle at an unknown site, and Boudica died soon after, by poison according to Tacitus or of illness according to Cassius Dio.",
 [('Only the Romans\' side','Everything written about the revolt comes from two Roman authors, Tacitus and Cassius Dio, writing decades or more than a century later; their figures, such as 70,000 killed in the towns and 80,000 Britons dead in the last battle against 400 Romans, are often treated as exaggerated.'),
  ('A national legend','Rediscovered in the Renaissance and celebrated by the Victorians as \'Boadicea\', she became a symbol of British resistance, with Thomas Thornycroft\'s statue unveiled beside Westminster Bridge in 1902; the location of the final battle and of her grave remain unknown, and local legends place her burial in many spots, including under a London railway station.')],
 ['wiki:Boudican_revolt','wiki:Boudica'],img='File:Boudica statue, Westminster (8433726848).jpg',end=61),
X('hadrians-wall','Hadrian\'s Wall (122 CE): the edge of the Roman world',H,'confirmed',1,122,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Hadrian's Wall was begun in 122 CE under the emperor Hadrian and ran about 117.5 km (73 miles) from Wallsend on the River Tyne to Bowness-on-Solway, across what is now northern England. In its final form it was a stone wall with ditches in front and behind, garrisoned by soldiers in large forts, milecastles and turrets. Twenty years later the turf-built Antonine Wall further north briefly replaced it before being abandoned. Much of its stone was later taken for roads and farmhouses. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987.",
 [('Defence or control?','The popular image is of a wall built to keep out the \'barbarian\' tribes of the north. Many historians argue its main roles were controlling and taxing movement, with gates that may have served as customs posts, and displaying Roman power, rather than stopping full-scale invasions.'),
  ('Not the border','A common misconception is that the wall marks the border between England and Scotland; it lies entirely in England, in places more than 100 km south of the modern border.')],
 ["wiki:Hadrian's_Wall",'wiki:Antonine_Wall'],img="File:Hadrian's Wall west of Housesteads 3.jpg"),
X('vindolanda-tablets','The Vindolanda tablets: socks, beer and a birthday party on Rome\'s frontier',H,'confirmed',2,100,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 1973 the archaeologist Robin Birley found thin wooden writing tablets with ink text at Vindolanda, a Roman fort just south of Hadrian's Wall. More than 1,700 have now been found, dating to the late 1st and early 2nd centuries CE, and they are among the oldest surviving handwritten documents from Britain. They record military business and private letters: a cavalry officer asks for more beer because his men have run out, a letter mentions socks and underpants sent to a soldier, a report calls the local Britons Brittunculi ('little Britons'), and Claudia Severa, wife of a nearby commander, invites her friend Sulpicia Lepidina to her birthday party in about 100 CE, adding a greeting in her own hand, perhaps the oldest known writing in Latin by a woman. Most of the tablets are held by the British Museum.",
 [('Survival by accident','The tablets survived because they were thrown away and sealed in waterlogged, oxygen-free layers; before 1973 historians did not know that wooden ink tablets of this kind had been used so widely.'),
  ('Voices usually missing','Historians value them because they record ordinary soldiers, families and enslaved people rather than emperors; some caution that they still reflect the world of the fort\'s literate officers.')],
 ['wiki:Vindolanda_tablets','wiki:Vindolanda'],img='Vindolanda tablets',dateNote='Tablets c. 85–130 CE; birthday invitation c. 100 CE; first found 1973'),
X('plague-of-cyprian','The Plague of Cyprian (249–262): a mystery pandemic in a Roman crisis',H,'confirmed',2,249,['textual','archaeological'],
 "From about 249 to 262 CE (or 251 to 270) a pandemic swept the Roman Empire during the Crisis of the Third Century. It is named after Cyprian, bishop of Carthage, who described it in his treatise On Mortality, including symptoms such as bleeding into the eyes. At its height 5,000 people a day were said to be dying in Rome, and one historian has calculated that the population of Alexandria fell from about 500,000 to 190,000, though people fleeing may account for part of that. In Thebes, Egypt, archaeologists found remains of bodies burned and covered in lime from this period. The disease has not been identified.",
 [('What disease?','Proposed causes include smallpox, measles and a viral haemorrhagic fever like Ebola; the historian Kyle Harper has argued for a filovirus, while others think the descriptions fit smallpox. Without ancient DNA, none is confirmed.'),
  ('Plague and faith','Cyprian told Christians the plague struck \'the just and unjust\' alike and urged them to care for the sick; the sociologist Rodney Stark and others argue that this care helped Christianity grow, a view some historians think overstates the evidence. The same years saw official persecutions of Christians under Decius and Valerian.')],
 ['wiki:Plague_of_Cyprian','wiki:Crisis_of_the_Third_Century'],img='Plague of Cyprian',end=262),
X('cai-lun-paper','Cai Lun and paper (105 CE): the "inventor" who improved an older craft',H,'confirmed',2,105,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Cai Lun was a eunuch official at the Eastern Han court in Luoyang. In 105 CE, according to the Book of the Later Han, he presented the emperor with an improved way of making paper from tree bark, hemp waste, old rags and fishing nets, which made large-scale paper production possible and was later carried across Asia and to Europe. Archaeological finds show that paper of other kinds had existed in China since at least the 2nd or 3rd century BCE, so his contribution was a major improvement rather than an invention. Caught up in court politics, he killed himself by poison around 121 CE. He was later deified as the god of papermaking.",
 [('Inventor or improver?','Traditional histories credited Cai Lun with inventing paper; the discovery of older paper fragments, some used for wrapping and at least one carrying a map, changed this view in the 20th century. Some Chinese scholars still emphasise his role as decisive.'),
  ('How paper reached the West','A famous story says Chinese papermakers captured at the Battle of Talas in 751 taught the craft in Samarkand; historians debate the story, noting that paper was probably already known in Central Asia earlier.')],
 ['wiki:Cai_Lun','wiki:History_of_paper'],img='Cai Lun',end=121),
X('council-of-nicaea','The Council of Nicaea (325): what it decided, and what it did not',H,'confirmed',1,325,['textual'],
 "In 325 CE the emperor Constantine I called a council of Christian bishops at Nicaea (now İznik, Turkey), the first 'ecumenical' council. Between about 250 and 300 bishops attended, nearly all from the eastern empire; the traditional figure of 318 matches the number of Abraham's servants in Genesis. The main dispute was the teaching of the Alexandrian priest Arius that the Son was created by and subordinate to the Father. The council rejected Arius' view, produced the first version of the Nicene Creed, set a common rule for the date of Easter and issued about twenty canons on church discipline.",
 [('A popular myth','There is no record of the council discussing which books belonged in the Bible; the biblical canon developed over a longer period. The idea that Nicaea chose the books seems to come largely from Voltaire, who repeated a story that the competing books were placed on an altar and the false ones fell off.'),
  ('Politics and belief','Some see Nicaea as the moment an emperor shaped Christian doctrine for political unity; others stress that the bishops debated theology that had divided churches for years, and that the Arian dispute continued for decades after the council, with Constantine himself later moving to readmit Arius.')],
 ['wiki:First_Council_of_Nicaea','wiki:Arianism','wiki:Development_of_the_Christian_biblical_canon'],img='First Council of Nicaea'),
X('apollonius-of-tyana','Apollonius of Tyana: the pagan miracle-worker compared with Jesus',H,'disputed',2,15,['textual'],
 "Apollonius of Tyana in Cappadocia (c. 15 – c. 100 CE) was a Greek philosopher of the Neopythagorean school who travelled and taught in the Middle East, North Africa and India and was one of the most famous wonder-workers of his age. Most of what is known comes from the long Life of Apollonius written about a century later by Philostratus at the request of the empress Julia Domna. It tells of healings, exorcisms, foreknowledge, raising a dead girl and vanishing from his trial before the emperor Domitian; his followers believed he was taken up to heaven. Most scholars accept that he was a real person.",
 [('A rival to Christ?','Around 300 CE the Roman official Hierocles used Apollonius\' miracles to argue that Jesus was not unique, and Eusebius of Caesarea wrote a reply; modern writers have sometimes claimed that the Gospels copied Apollonius or vice versa. Scholars generally see both as examples of a wider ancient type of holy man, and note that Philostratus wrote after the Gospels.'),
  ('Novel or biography?','Many historians treat Philostratus\' Life as partly a novel, with travel tales such as meetings with Indian sages; others think it preserves real details from older sources now lost.')],
 ['wiki:Apollonius_of_Tyana','wiki:Life_of_Apollonius_of_Tyana'],img='Apollonius of Tyana',end=100,statusNote='Person historical; miracle stories legendary'),
X('simon-magus','Simon Magus: the sorcerer who flew over Rome',M,'myth-legend',2,35,['textual','mythological'],
 "Simon Magus was a Samaritan of the 1st century CE known almost entirely from Christian sources. In the Acts of the Apostles he is a magician who is baptised but then tries to buy the power to give the Holy Spirit, which gave the Church the word simony for buying church offices. Later apocryphal texts, such as the Acts of Peter, make him a great sorcerer who could levitate and fly: in Rome he rose into the air above the Forum to prove he was a god, until Peter prayed and he fell onto the Sacred Way, breaking his leg in three places. Second-century Church Fathers called him the founder of Gnosticism and 'father of all heresies'.",
 [('A misread statue','Justin Martyr (2nd century) wrote that Romans had set up a statue to Simon inscribed \'Simoni Deo Sancto\' (\'to Simon the holy god\'). In the 16th century a statue base was found on the Tiber Island inscribed \'Semoni Sanco Deo Fidio\', a dedication to the Sabine god Semo Sancus; many scholars think Justin misread a similar inscription.'),
  ('Founder of Gnosticism?','Some modern scholars accept the Church Fathers\' picture of Simon as a Gnostic teacher with followers (the Simonians); others argue that the heresiologists projected later movements back onto him, and that the real Simon is almost impossible to recover.')],
 ['wiki:Simon_Magus','wiki:Simony'],img='Simon Magus',dateNote='Mid-1st century CE; legends written in the 2nd–3rd centuries'),
X('thomas-in-india','Did the apostle Thomas reach India?',H,'disputed',2,52,['textual','oral tradition','numismatic'],
 "The Saint Thomas Christians of Kerala hold that the apostle Thomas landed at Muziris on the Malabar coast in 52 CE, founded seven churches, and was killed near Mylapore (now part of Chennai) in 72 CE; San Thome Basilica stands over the traditional tomb. The main early text, the apocryphal Acts of Thomas (written in Syria around 180–230 CE), says Thomas was sold as a slave to the Indian king Gudnaphar. For a long time this king was assumed to be fictional, until coins and an inscription of an Indo-Parthian ruler named Gondophares, who ruled in what is now Afghanistan and Pakistan in the 1st century CE, were identified in the 19th century.",
 [('Tradition','For the Saint Thomas Christians the mission is a living tradition, and Roman trade with Muziris in the 1st century shows that a journey from the eastern Mediterranean to south India was possible.'),
  ('Scholarly doubt','Many historians regard the story as unprovable, noting that the Acts of Thomas is a legendary text and that Gondophares ruled far to the north of Kerala; some suggest the community was founded or strengthened by later Syriac Christian traders and missionaries, such as Thomas of Cana, whom tradition dates to the 4th or 8th century.')],
 ['wiki:Thomas_the_Apostle','wiki:Saint_Thomas_Christians','wiki:Gondophares'],img='Thomas the Apostle',end=72,statusNote='Gondophares confirmed; the mission itself unproven'),
X('lady-of-cao','The Lady of Cao: a tattooed woman ruler of the Moche',H,'confirmed',2,450,['archaeological','bioarchaeological'],
 "In 2006 Peruvian archaeologists led by Régulo Franco found the mummified body of a woman at El Brujo, a Moche site about 45 km north of Trujillo in northern Peru. She had died in her mid-twenties around 450 CE, possibly from complications of pregnancy or childbirth. Her arms and feet were tattooed with designs including snakes and spiders, and she was buried with rich ornaments, crowns and war clubs, together with the body of a young woman who may have been sacrificed. Before the find, Moche rulers were assumed to have been men. In 2017 a reconstruction of her face was unveiled.",
 [('Ruler or priestess?','The weapons and regalia lead many archaeologists to see her as a ruler, perhaps of the Chicama valley; others prefer high priestess, since Moche art shows women in ritual roles and weapons can be symbolic.'),
  ('Rewriting the Moche','Later discoveries of other high-status women at San José de Moro suggest that women held powerful positions more widely in Moche society than once thought.')],
 ['wiki:Lady_of_Cao','wiki:Moche_culture'],img='Lady of Cao'),
X('attila-the-hun','Attila the Hun: the Scourge of God and his hidden grave',H,'confirmed',1,434,['textual'],
 "Attila ruled the Huns from 434 (jointly with his brother Bleda until 445) until his death in 453, at the head of an empire of Huns, Ostrogoths, Alans, Gepids and others in central and eastern Europe. He ravaged the Balkans but could not take Constantinople, invaded Gaul in 451 and was halted at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains, and in 452 invaded Italy; at the River Mincio he met an embassy including Pope Leo I and agreed to withdraw. According to the historian Priscus he died on the night of his wedding to a young woman named Ildico, apparently choking on a nosebleed in a drunken stupor. His empire collapsed soon after.",
 [('The lost grave','The 6th-century historian Jordanes says Attila was buried secretly at night in three coffins of gold, silver and iron, and that the men who buried him were killed so the place would never be known. The grave has never been found.'),
  ('Murder?','Some later chroniclers, such as Marcellinus Comes, claimed Ildico stabbed him; modern historians usually prefer Priscus\' account. Attila also lived on in legend as Etzel in the Nibelungenlied and Atli in Norse sagas, and the credit given to Pope Leo for saving Rome is debated, since famine and disease in Attila\'s army may also explain his retreat.')],
 ['wiki:Attila','wiki:Huns'],img='File:Attila-ChroniconPictum.jpg',end=453),
X('sack-of-rome-410','The sack of Rome (410): the fall that inspired the City of God',H,'confirmed',1,410,['textual','archaeological'],
 "On 24 August 410 the Visigoths under their king Alaric entered Rome through the Salarian Gate and plundered the city for three days. Rome was no longer the empire's capital, which had moved to Milan and then Ravenna, but it was the first time in almost 800 years that the city had fallen to a foreign enemy. The emperor's sister Galla Placidia was taken captive. Jerome, in Bethlehem, wrote that 'the city which had taken the whole world was itself taken'. The shock led Augustine of Hippo to write The City of God, answering pagans who blamed the disaster on Rome's abandonment of the old gods for Christianity.",
 [('Alaric\'s river grave','According to Jordanes, Alaric died soon afterwards near Cosenza in southern Italy and was buried with treasure in the bed of the River Busento, which was diverted and then returned to its course; the captives who did the work were killed. Treasure hunters have searched for the grave, but it has never been found.'),
  ('How great a turning point?','The sack is often treated as a symbol of the fall of the Western Roman Empire; some historians stress that it was relatively restrained, that churches were spared, and that the western empire lasted until 476.')],
 ['wiki:Sack_of_Rome_(410)','wiki:Alaric_I','wiki:The_City_of_God'],img='Sack of Rome (410)'),
]
# --- part 3: modern myths, doomsdays, pathological science and folklore
E+=[
X('georgia-guidestones','The Georgia Guidestones: a granite guide for after the apocalypse (1980–2022)',H,'confirmed',2,1980,['documentary','archaeoastronomical'],
 "In June 1979 a man using the pseudonym Robert C. Christian commissioned a monument from the Elberton Granite Finishing Company in Georgia, USA, saying he represented 'a small group of loyal Americans who believe in God'. Erected on 22 March 1980, the Georgia Guidestones were six granite slabs about 5.9 m tall carrying ten guidelines for a world after a coming calamity, in eight modern languages, beginning 'Maintain humanity under 500,000,000 in perpetual balance with nature'. A hole drilled through the central pillar showed the North Star and a slot was aligned with the solstices and equinoxes. On 6 July 2022 the stones were badly damaged by a bomb and were removed the same day. The sponsor's identity was never officially confirmed.",
 [('Conspiracy readings','Because of the population figure and the anonymous sponsor, the stones became the subject of conspiracy theories linking them to a secret elite plan or to Satanism; in 2022 a candidate for governor of Georgia called them satanic and promised to remove them. No evidence of such a plan has been produced.'),
  ('A time capsule of Cold War fears','Others see the monument as a product of late-1970s anxieties about nuclear war and overpopulation, and as a modern counterpart to ancient monuments with astronomical alignments; a 2015 documentary claimed to expose the sponsor, but the claim has not been confirmed.')],
 ['wiki:Georgia_Guidestones'],img='Georgia Guidestones',end=2022),
X('montauk-project','The Montauk Project: time travel at an abandoned radar base?',H,'debunked',3,1983,['testimony','speculative'],
 "The Montauk Project is a conspiracy theory claiming that the US government ran secret experiments at Camp Hero, a former military base and radar station at Montauk on Long Island, New York, including mind control, teleportation, time travel and contact with extraterrestrials, as a sequel to the alleged 1943 Philadelphia Experiment. The stories circulated from the early 1980s and were spread by a series of books by Preston Nichols and Peter Moon. Nichols and Al Bielek said they had recovered repressed memories of their own involvement. The base and its large radar tower are real and now part of a state park; no documentary evidence supports the experiments.",
 [('Recovered memories','The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée described the accounts as highly questionable; critics note that recovered-memory testimony is unreliable and that Bielek\'s biography contains claims that have been disproved.'),
  ('Why it endures','A closed Cold War base with a giant radar dish invites speculation; the story inspired the TV series Stranger Things, which had the working title Montauk.')],
 ['wiki:Montauk_Project','wiki:Camp_Hero_State_Park'],img='File:Camp Hero Radar Tower.jpg',dateNote='Stories from the early 1980s; first book 1992',statusNote='Camp Hero is real; the secret experiments are unsupported'),
X('dogu-figurines','The goggle-eyed dogū: Jōmon goddesses or ancient astronauts?',H,'confirmed',2,-1000,['archaeological','speculative'],
 "Dogū are small clay figures made in Japan during the Jōmon period; an estimated 15,000–18,000 are known, mostly from eastern Japan, and none were made after the Jōmon. Most appear female, with large eyes, small waists, wide hips and sometimes pregnant bellies. The best-known type, the shakōki-dogū or 'goggle-eyed' figures of about 1000–400 BCE from sites such as Kamegaoka in Aomori, has huge eyes that reminded early scholars of Arctic snow goggles. Many dogū were found deliberately broken.",
 [('Archaeological view','Scholars interpret dogū as figures connected with fertility, childbirth or healing; one theory is that illness was transferred into a figure that was then broken. No single purpose has been proven.'),
  ('Ancient astronauts (speculative)','In Chariots of the Gods? (1968) Erich von Däniken presented the goggle-eyed dogū as astronauts in spacesuits and helmets, an idea that became famous in Japan and abroad. Archaeologists reject it, noting that the figures belong to a long Jōmon tradition of stylised human forms.')],
 ['wiki:Dogū','wiki:Chariots_of_the_Gods?','doi:10.1177/14696053241292579'],img='Dogū',end=-400,dateNote='Goggle-eyed type c. 1000–400 BCE; dogū as a whole span much of the Jōmon period',statusNote='Figurines real; spacesuit reading speculative'),
X('polywater','Polywater (1961–1973): the new form of water that was sweat',H,'debunked',2,1961,['experimental','textual'],
 "In 1961 the Soviet physicist Nikolai Fedyakin reported that water condensed in very narrow quartz tubes seemed to have a higher boiling point, lower freezing point and a syrupy viscosity. Boris Derjaguin of Moscow's Institute for Physical Chemistry developed the work, and by 1969 Western laboratories were trying to reproduce 'polywater' while newspapers worried about a 'polywater gap' with the USSR. Doubts grew from 1970: Denis Rousseau and Sergio Porto of Bell Labs found the samples were mostly contaminated with sodium and chlorine, and Rousseau showed his own sweat after a handball game had the same properties. By 1973 polywater was recognised as ordinary water contaminated with dissolved substances and tiny particles of silica and other materials.",
 [('Pathological science','Polywater is often cited, alongside N-rays and cold fusion, as a case of pathological science, in which researchers see an effect they hope to see at the limits of detection; Rousseau himself later wrote about such cases.'),
  ('Science correcting itself','Others point out that the episode lasted barely a decade and was ended by careful replication and analysis, showing the scientific process working, if slowly, under Cold War pressure.')],
 ['wiki:Polywater','doi:10.1126/science.167.3926.1715','wiki:Pathological_science'],img='Polywater',end=1973),
X('planet-vulcan','Vulcan: the planet inside Mercury\'s orbit that Einstein made vanish',H,'debunked',2,1859,['astronomical observation','textual'],
 "In 1859 the French mathematician Urbain Le Verrier, who had predicted Neptune from the motion of Uranus, showed that Mercury's orbit shifted slightly more than Newtonian physics allowed and proposed an unseen planet or ring of asteroids closer to the Sun. That December a country doctor and amateur astronomer, Edmond Lescarbault, wrote that he had seen a small round body cross the Sun on 26 March 1859; Le Verrier visited him, was convinced, and announced the planet, named Vulcan. Searches over the following decades produced conflicting claims, including sightings by James Craig Watson and Lewis Swift during the total eclipse of 29 July 1878. In 1915 Einstein's general theory of relativity explained Mercury's orbit through the curvature of spacetime, and no planet was needed.",
 [('Why it was believed','The same method had just found Neptune, so a planet near the Sun was a reasonable prediction; observers looking into the glare near the Sun could easily mistake stars, sunspots or instrument effects for a planet.'),
  ('Small bodies still possible','Astronomers have since searched for tiny \'vulcanoid\' asteroids in stable orbits close to the Sun; none has been found, though very small ones are not ruled out.')],
 ['wiki:Vulcan_(hypothetical_planet)','wiki:Tests_of_general_relativity'],img='Vulcan (hypothetical planet)',end=1915),
X('nibiru-cataclysm','The Nibiru cataclysm: a doomsday planet that keeps not arriving',H,'debunked',2,1995,['speculative','textual'],
 "The idea that a large planet called Nibiru or Planet X would collide with or pass close to Earth was first put forward in 1995 by Nancy Lieder, founder of the website ZetaTalk, who said she received messages from aliens of the Zeta Reticuli system through a brain implant. She predicted the encounter for May 2003, a date later postponed. The claim spread through internet doomsday groups and in the late 2000s merged with the 2012 phenomenon; in 2017 the self-described Christian numerologist David Meade predicted it for 23 September 2017. The name comes from the ancient-astronaut writer Zecharia Sitchin's readings of Babylonian texts, though he denied any link to the doomsday claims. Astronomers, including NASA scientists, have repeatedly stated that no such planet exists; an object large enough would long since have been visible.",
 [('Nibiru in the texts','In Babylonian astronomy Nibiru was a name associated with the god Marduk and usually with a visible planet such as Jupiter, or a point in the sky; Assyriologists reject Sitchin\'s reading of it as a hidden twelfth planet.'),
  ('A recurring pattern','Each failed date has been followed by a new one, often tied to a real astronomical news story such as Comet ISON or the hypothesised Planet Nine, a pattern seen in other millenarian predictions.')],
 ['wiki:Nibiru_cataclysm','wiki:Zecharia_Sitchin'],dateNote='Claim first made 1995; predicted dates 2003, 2012, 2017 and later'),
X('y2k-bug','Y2K (2000): the doomsday that was fixed, or never coming?',H,'confirmed',2,2000,['documentary','technical'],
 "Many computer programs stored years with only two digits, so that 2000 would be read as 1900. In the late 1990s fears grew that banking, power, transport and military systems could fail at midnight on 31 December 1999; some people stocked food, water, guns and generators and withdrew cash. Governments and companies spent an estimated 300 billion US dollars worldwide on checking and fixing software. When the date rolled over, few major failures occurred.",
 [('A success','One view holds that the problem was real and that the remediation work, much of it unseen, prevented serious failures; supporters point to errors that were caught in testing and to minor glitches that did occur in 2000.'),
  ('An overreaction','Another view holds that the danger was exaggerated by consultants and the media, noting that countries and small businesses that spent little on preparation had few problems. Both views are still argued.')],
 ['wiki:Year_2000_problem'],img='Year 2000 problem',dateNote='Rollover 1 January 2000; remediation through the 1990s'),
X('owlman-1976','The Owlman of Mawnan (1976): a bird-man over a Cornish church',H,'disputed',3,1976,['testimony'],
 "On 17 April 1976 two young sisters on holiday in Mawnan, Cornwall, were reported to have seen a large winged 'feathered bird-man' hovering above the tower of the parish church, and their father cut the holiday short. The story was reported by Tony 'Doc' Shiels, a showman and magician who was involved that year in 'monster-raising' stunts, and who said one girl gave him a drawing of what he called the Owlman. Shiels reported a second sighting by two 14-year-old girls on 3 July 1976, and scattered reports followed in 1978, 1979, 1989 and 1995.",
 [('A barn owl','Sceptics suggest misidentification of a large owl such as a barn owl, a species known to nest in church towers.'),
  ('A hoax','The occult historian Gareth Medway and others suggest the whole story may have been a hoax by Shiels, who was also linked to photographs of the sea monster Morgawr.'),
  ('Cryptid (speculative)','Some cryptozoologists treat the Owlman as a genuine unknown creature, comparing it with Mothman in West Virginia (1966–67).')],
 ['wiki:Owlman'],img='Owlman'),
X('men-in-black','The Men in Black: silencers of UFO witnesses',M,'myth-legend',2,1953,['testimony','folklore'],
 "In UFO lore, the men in black are mysterious figures in dark suits, often in threes, who visit witnesses and pressure them to keep quiet about what they saw. The idea goes back to Albert K. Bender, who in 1953 closed his International Flying Saucer Bureau saying he had been visited and warned by three men in dark suits; the writer Gray Barker spread the story in his 1956 book They Knew Too Much About Flying Saucers. John Keel later described MIB as uncanny, almost supernatural visitors. The legend inspired the Men in Black comics and films from 1997.",
 [('Government agents','Some ufologists believe the visitors were agents of the US government or military trying to suppress information; officials did sometimes interview witnesses, but no evidence of an organised campaign of intimidation has been found.'),
  ('Folklore and hoax','The folklorist James R. Lewis compared MIB accounts with traditional tales of meeting the Devil, and John C. Sherwood later wrote that Gray Barker had urged him, as a teenager in the late 1960s, to create a hoax MIB story that Barker then published.')],
 ['wiki:Men_in_black','wiki:Gray_Barker'],img='Men in black',dateNote='Bender story 1953; popularised 1956'),
X('slender-man','Slender Man (2009): a monster invented on a forum that escaped into the world',M,'myth-legend',2,2009,['documentary','folklore'],
 "Slender Man was created in June 2009 by Eric Knudsen, posting as 'Victor Surge' on the Something Awful forum, for a contest to make fake paranormal photographs. His tall, faceless figure in a black suit, lurking behind children, was quickly taken up by other users, who added fake historical sources, including a story set in 16th-century Germany, and he spread through web series such as Marble Hornets and video games. In 2014 two 12-year-old girls in Waukesha, Wisconsin, stabbed a classmate, saying they wanted to please Slender Man, and a moral panic followed; the victim survived.",
 [('Folklore in real time','Folklorists such as Shira Chess describe Slender Man as a rare chance to watch a legend form, and link him to older stories of fairies who steal children; the invented old woodcuts and documents mimic the way older legends gained authority.'),
  ('Legend tripping and harm','Researchers on the Waukesha case stress that the girls\' mental health was central, while others discuss how online legends can blur the line between fiction and belief.')],
 ['wiki:Slender_Man','wiki:Slender_Man_stabbing'],img='Slender Man',statusNote='Known modern fiction; the 2014 stabbing is fact'),
X('heitsi-eibib','Heitsi-eibib: the Khoikhoi hero who died and rose again, and his stone cairns',M,'myth-legend',3,1881,['oral tradition','ethnographic records'],
 "Heitsi-eibib (also Haitse-aibeb) is a hero and trickster of Khoikhoi tradition in southern Africa, who, with Tsui-ǁGoab and Gaunab, is a central figure of their myths. He is a shapeshifter who appears as a man or a child, is said to have died and come back to life many times, and is not always well behaved. In one story he defeats the monster Ga-Gorib, who sat beside a deep hole and challenged passers-by to throw stones at him, the stones bouncing back to kill them; Heitsi-eibib refused, distracted him, struck him behind the ear and knocked him into his own pit. Many stone cairns in South Africa and Namibia are known as his graves.",
 [('Graves of a myth','The many cairns called Heitsi-eibib\'s graves tie a mythical figure to real places in the landscape, a pattern also seen in the graves of giants and heroes in Europe and elsewhere; who built each cairn, and when, is usually unknown.'),
  ('Comparisons','Nineteenth-century collectors compared his repeated deaths and returns with dying-and-rising figures in other religions; modern scholars warn that such comparisons can impose outside frameworks on Khoikhoi belief, which was recorded mostly by missionaries and colonial writers.')],
 ['wiki:Heitsi-eibib','wiki:Khoikhoi'],dateNote='Recorded in the 19th century; tradition much older'),
X('lost-city-kalahari','The Lost City of the Kalahari: a showman\'s ruins that turned out to be rock',H,'debunked',2,1885,['textual','geological','travel account'],
 "In 1885 William Leonard Hunt, a Canadian tightrope walker and showman known as 'the Great Farini', crossed part of the Kalahari Desert and on his return published a book describing what he took to be the ruins of a long-lost city of stone. A paper was read in his absence to the Royal Geographical Society and photographs from his trip were exhibited. The story became a South African legend, compared with Great Zimbabwe, and inspired many searches; numerous expeditions set out in the 1930s. In 1964 the researcher Clement, working with F. R. Paver, studied Farini's route, concluded he had never reached the heart of the Kalahari, and found along the probable route a formation of dolerite rocks that erodes into straight, regular blocks resembling walls.",
 [('A natural formation','Most researchers accept Clement\'s conclusion that Farini saw natural dolerite outcrops, possibly embellished by a showman\'s eye for a good story.'),
  ('Still searched','Some explorers continue to look for a real ruined city elsewhere in the Kalahari, and television programmes have revisited the \'Clement walls\'; no archaeological city has been found.')],
 ['wiki:Lost_City_of_the_Kalahari','wiki:William_Leonard_Hunt'],img="File:South Africa- Mr. G.A. Farini's Routes in the Kalahari Desert - DPLA - 314980487fd2555a44ce0ed60c4af43f.jpg",dateNote='Farini\'s journey 1885; Clement\'s study 1964'),
X('kruger-millions','The Kruger Millions: the Boer gold that vanished in 1900',H,'disputed',2,1900,['documentary','oral tradition'],
 "In May–June 1900, as British forces approached Pretoria during the Second Boer War, President Paul Kruger's government left the capital. On 4 June the State Attorney Jan Smuts had the gold in the Mint and the National Bank weighed and loaded onto a train east along the Delagoa Bay line. Kruger later left for Europe via Lourenço Marques (Maputo), and a diary records gold being handed to a German firm there in August 1900. British records and later calculations suggest a large amount of gold was never accounted for, giving rise to the legend of the 'Kruger Millions': a hoard buried somewhere in the Lowveld or shipped abroad.",
 [('Buried in the bushveld','Many stories place the gold in hiding places along the railway or in the hills of Mpumalanga, and treasure hunters still search; no hoard has been found.'),
  ('Spent or exported','Other accounts hold that the gold was used to fund the Boer war effort and Kruger\'s exile, or was shipped to Europe, and that the missing quantities reflect gaps in the records rather than buried treasure.')],
 ['wiki:Kruger_Millions','wiki:Paul_Kruger'],img='Kruger Millions',statusNote='Removal of the gold documented; a hidden hoard unproven'),
X('daedalus-sea-serpent','The HMS Daedalus sea serpent (1848): a navy captain\'s monster',H,'disputed',2,1848,['testimony','textual'],
 "On 6 August 1848 Captain Peter M'Quhae of the Royal Navy frigate HMS Daedalus, sailing for Saint Helena, and several officers and crew reported a huge serpent-like animal swimming past the ship, with its head held above the water, in view for about twenty minutes. The captain said it passed so close that he could have recognised a man's features. His report, published in The Times, caused a sensation, and the anatomist Richard Owen publicly argued that the crew had seen a large seal. Earlier, from August 1817, hundreds of people at Gloucester, Massachusetts, reported a sea serpent off Cape Ann, investigated by the Linnaean Society of New England.",
 [('Known animals','Proposed explanations include a large seal, a sei whale (argued by Galbreath), a giant squid, floating seaweed, or the oarfish, a very long fish that may lie behind many sea-serpent sightings.'),
  ('An unknown animal (speculative)','Some cryptozoologists argue that consistent reports by experienced sailors point to an undiscovered large marine animal; no specimen has been found.')],
 ['wiki:Sea_serpent','wiki:Gloucester_sea_serpent'],img='File:The Great Sea-Serpent - ILN 1848.jpg',dateNote='6 August 1848; Gloucester sightings from 1817'),
X('aswang','The aswang: shapeshifting ghouls of the Philippines, and a Cold War trick',M,'myth-legend',2,1589,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Aswang is a general Filipino name for malevolent shapeshifting creatures: vampires, ghouls, witches, viscera-suckers and beings that turn into dogs, cats or pigs. Spanish friars in the 16th century, including Juan de Plasencia in 1589, recorded beliefs in such beings, including the manananggal, a creature that appeared at night without its head or entrails, and noted that the aswang was the most feared of all. The belief is strongest in parts of the Visayas, especially the province of Capiz, often called the aswang's home.",
 [('A weapon of psychological war','In the early 1950s the US officer Edward Lansdale, advising the Philippine government against the Huk rebellion, spread rumours that aswangs were loose in the jungle; his men then drained the blood from a captured rebel\'s body and left it to be found, and the Huks fled the area. The tactic had earlier been used by the Japanese army in the Second World War.'),
  ('Social meaning','Scholars suggest the aswang\'s behaviour can be read as an inversion of traditional Filipino values, a mirror of what a community fears, much as witch beliefs work elsewhere.')],
 ['wiki:Aswang','wiki:Edward_Lansdale'],img='Aswang',dateNote='Recorded by Spanish friars in the 16th century; tradition older'),
X('tikbalang','The tikbalang: the horse-headed trickster of Philippine forests',M,'myth-legend',3,1589,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "The tikbalang of Philippine folklore is a tall, bony creature with the head and hooves of a horse and limbs so long that its knees rise above its head when it squats. It haunts mountains and forests and leads travellers astray so that they walk in circles; wearing one's shirt inside out is said to break the spell. When the sun shines during rain, people say a tikbalang is getting married. Early Spanish sources, such as Juan de Plasencia's account of Tagalog customs (1589), described tigbalang only as forest ghosts or spirits; since horses were not native to the islands before the Spanish, the horse form appears to have been added later.",
 [('A borrowed shape?','Folklorists note the horse form probably developed after the arrival of horses, while the older idea of a wild forest spirit is pre-colonial; the sunshower saying has close parallels in other cultures, such as Japan\'s fox\'s wedding.'),
  ('A real experience','Some writers link stories of being led in circles to genuine disorientation in dense forest; for believers, the tikbalang remains a real presence.')],
 ['wiki:Tikbalang'],img='Tikbalang',dateNote='Recorded from 1589; horse form later'),
]
